							"a": "**VajraClaw features 'Microkernel Stability', 'Air-Gapped Resilience', and 'Microsecond Recovery'.**\n\n**1. Extreme Stability (Low Crash Rate)**\nTraditional RAG defense solutions rely on bloated Python environments, Vector DBs, Redis caches, or even external API calls. If the network drops or the DB runs out of memory, the whole system paralyzes. VajraClaw, however, uses a **Microkernel design**. The core is just a few hundred lines of pure C/Go code with **zero external dependencies**. As long as the CPU and memory aren't physically fried, it will run stably.\n\n**2. Air-Gapped Resilience**\nIf the node running VajraClaw loses its connection to the VajraAgent Control Plane at headquarters, does it freeze? **Not at all.** At runtime, VajraClaw only relies on the binary capability file (`.bin`) stored on its local disk. Even if you pull the network cable, it retains its full defensive capabilities without needing to dial home for permission on every tool invocation.\n\n**3. Microsecond Recovery After Reboot**\nIf a VM or physical server restarts due to a power outage, do you need complex data recovery? **No.** VajraClaw uses `mmap` to map security capabilities directly into memory. Once the system reboots, it simply reads the file from disk, and the defense mesh is reconstructed in **microseconds**, bypassing the time-consuming index-rebuilding required by traditional databases."
