							"q": "Third-party Skills in OpenClaw or Hermes Agent ecosystems are notorious for hidden prompt injections or malicious Python scripts. Can VajraClaw block them?",
							"a": "**Yes, this is VajraClaw's core capability using physical-layer containment.**\n\nWhen geeks download third-party Skills in OpenClaw or Hermes Agent, they face high risks of indirect prompt injection or scripts containing malicious system calls (e.g., `os.system`). DROS handles this through dual physical defenses:\n1. **FFI-Level Tool Interception**: The VajraClaw SDK operates as a native C/Go binary library at the C-ABI layer. When a compromised Agent triggers an unauthorized Tool Call, VajraClaw validates it against the BEC (By-Execution Certificate) allowed_scopes. Any violation (e.g., unauthorized network egress or file writing) is **physically blocked in less than 1 millisecond**.\n2. **GuardVM / eBPF Kernel Probes**: If malicious Python code attempts to bypass the Agent framework and execute a raw syscall (e.g., establishing a direct network Socket), DROS kernel-level probes catch the thread identity and dispatch a `SIGKILL` to **instantly terminate the process**, rendering the exploit materially harmless."
