							"q": "How does DROS defend against 'cascade infection' and 'privilege escalation' in Multi-Agent workflows (e.g., AutoGen, CrewAI)? What is the performance overhead?",
							"a": "**DROS introduces the cryptographic BEC Chain (By-Execution Certificate Chain) with intersection authorization to eliminate the Confused Deputy problem, with practically zero performance overhead.**\n\n- **BEC Chain Intersection**: Inter-agent communication requires passing cryptographically signed capability tokens. When Agent B (high-privilege) receives a request from Agent A (low-privilege), VajraClaw calculates the effective privilege boundary on the fly:\n  $$\\text{Effective Scope} = \\text{Scope}(Agent_A) \\cap \\text{Scope}(Agent_B)$$\n  If Agent A (web crawler) is compromised and commands Agent B (database manager) to purge a database, the intersection is evaluated as empty, and VajraClaw melts the request instantly, preventing privilege escalation.\n- **Microsecond Latency**: VajraClaw's Go/C implementation leverages an **O(1) Bitmap state machine algorithm** for local policy evaluation. A single rule check takes only **10 to 50 microseconds**, with **zero token cost** (fully executed in local memory). The performance impact is materially negligible compared to LLM inference times."
